% groundings.bib — one entry per TERM-to-WORK mapping.
%
% ⚑ WHAT A GROUNDING IS.  A claim that *this recorded work is what industry practitioners call X*.
% The operator's framing: "We're not looking for anything novel.  We're looking to ground everything
% in what other industry professionals might have called it, since I didn't know the terminology and
% may still not."  So this is a VOCABULARY layer over the concept library, not a novelty audit.
%
% ⚠⚠ AND IT IS A CLAIM ABOUT NAMING, WHICH CAN BE WRONG BY BEING ADJACENT.  `checks/grounding.py`
% verifies the PROVENANCE of the naming — that the `names` keys resolve in library/concepts.py, that
% the `rests-on` reference exists and carries a url or doi, that `verdict` is one of the two allowed
% values.  It CANNOT verify the term is apt.  Read the check's docstring before reading a green gate
% as the industry agreeing.
%
% ⚑ WHY THE `NEARLY` ENTRIES ARE HERE AT ALL, AND WHY THEY CARRY THE MOST VALUE.  Accuracy binds
% harder than coverage: a term that is adjacent-but-not-accurate is WORSE than no term, because
% adopting it is exactly the drift this corpus exists to prevent.  A NEARLY entry names the term,
% then says what DIFFERS — so the difference survives instead of being quietly rounded off the next
% time somebody reaches for the nearest available word.
%
% ⚠ TERMS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and each absence is a decision:
%   * "Air-gapped" for the Chef Policyfile.  A forward research pass returned it; the storage
%     lexicon's air-gapped means a backup copy unreachable from the production control plane, and
%     there is no air-gapped backup anywhere in this corpus.  MASTERLESS is the honest name and it
%     is what a Chef practitioner says.  See `g-masterless-chef`, the correction.
%   * "Hermetic" as a SLSA term — SLSA v1.0 explicitly declines to define it.
%   * "Data residency" / "data classification" / "data minimization" — the natural vocabulary for
%     the GDPR work, which is NDA-bound and permanently out of scope.  These would smuggle it in
%     under a generic-sounding name.
%   * "Managed service" / "cloud-native" / IaaS / FaaS / serverless — every one invites the AWS/GCP
%     codelab-only trap, and an ATS scan does not parse negation, so even a disclaimer is forbidden.
%   * Document-store / NoSQL vocabulary — MongoDB is a recurring fabrication and was never his.
%   * MDT / SCCM / task sequence / GPO / PowerShell JEA — see the collision in the check's docstring.
%   * "Error budget", "load shedding", "consistent hashing", "CAP/PACELC", "model checking" — no
%     work in this corpus instantiates them, so there is nothing to ground.

% ══ Networking and Traffic Engineering ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@misc{g-elephant-flows,
  section  = {networking},
  term     = {elephant flows},
  names    = {vi-lag-hashing-limit},
  verdict  = {APPLIES},
  rests-on = {r-rfc7424 r-benson-imc2010 r-hedera-nsdi2010},
  claim    = {\textbf{Elephant flows, and RFC 7424 is the primary citation rather than Hedera.} Hedera and CONGA address ECMP across a Clos fabric; this was a LAG on a single inter-switch link, which is RFC 7424 literal subject matter. The RFC states the three conditions under which hash-based distribution works --- a fairly uniform hash across flows, "the individual flow rates are much smaller as compared to the link capacity", and no dramatic rate differences --- and defines a large flow by example as one "at or above 10\% of link bandwidth for a time period of at least one second". A handful of fat, long-lived heal flows violates the second and third conditions at once: too few flows to average out, each large relative to the member. \textbf{Which is also why adding links does not fix it:} with few large flows, more members change WHICH member collides, not whether one does, and every remedy the RFC offers is a PLACEMENT mechanism rather than a capacity one},
  check    = {grounding:g-elephant-flows}
}

@misc{g-flow-polarization,
  section  = {networking},
  term     = {flow polarization},
  names    = {vi-lag-hashing-limit},
  verdict  = {APPLIES},
  rests-on = {r-juniper-hashing},
  claim    = {\textbf{Flow polarization.} The observation that on an inter-switch link the imbalance compounds instead of averaging is the vendor-named failure mode: identical hash inputs at successive stages concentrate flows rather than spreading them, which is why LAG and ECMP deliberately take different offsets of the same seed. The corpus derives the mechanism; the industry has a one-word name for it},
  check    = {grounding:g-flow-polarization}
}

@misc{g-hol-blocking,
  section  = {networking},
  term     = {head-of-line blocking},
  names    = {vi-lag-hashing-limit},
  verdict  = {NEARLY},
  rests-on = {r-rfc9113},
  claim    = {\textbf{Head-of-line blocking --- gloss it, do not claim it bare.} Flows pinned to a saturated LAG member while sibling members idle IS head-of-line blocking, but at the LINK-SELECTION layer. Canonical usage is per-queue or per-stream, as in HTTP/2's transport-level case. The phenomenon is the same and the layer is not, so the honest form is "head-of-line blocking at the link-selection layer" --- never the bare term, which a reader will resolve to the transport sense},
  check    = {grounding:g-hol-blocking}
}

